          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My child has a temperature of 39.5 and a rash that does not go pale when I press a glass on it. How urgent is this, and what should I do right now?",
          "answer": "**This is an emergency. Call emergency services now.** The assessment tool rated this as an emergency. A fever with a rash that does not fade under a glass can be a sign of meningitis or meningococcal infection, which can get worse within hours. Do not wait to see if it improves.\n\n**What to do right now:**\n- Call your local emergency number (for example 911 or 999) or go to the nearest emergency department now.\n- Tell the call handler exactly what you told me: a 39.5 C fever and a rash that does not fade when pressed.\n- Do not wait for more spots to appear, and do not wait to see whether the fever drops.\n- If your child becomes harder to wake, has a seizure, or has trouble breathing, say so immediately.\n\n**Other warning signs to look for (from the red-flag guidance):**\n- In babies and young children: a high-pitched or moaning cry, irritability when held, refusing feeds, a bulging soft spot on the head, floppiness or stiffness, unusual drowsiness, or pale, blotchy or mottled skin. (Source: *Signs suggesting meningitis or meningococcal infection*.)\n- Confusion, drowsiness, or difficulty waking. (Same source.)\n- Very fast breathing, a fast heartbeat, cold or mottled hands and feet, or not passing urine for many hours. (Source: *Sepsis: signs of a life-threatening response to infection*.)\n- Severe dislike of bright light, or a severe headache with a stiff neck, if your child can tell you. (Source: *Signs suggesting meningitis or meningococcal infection*.)\n\nYou do not need to confirm any of these signs before calling. The rash alone is reason enough to get emergency care now.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
